DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
The critical reception of Kautsky's philosophy of history divides broadly
into two phases. The first, spanning roughly 1920-1980, is dominated by
Western Marxist and Leninist condemnation. Lukacs in History and Class
Consciousness (1923) attacked the "contemplative" and scientistic character
of Second International Marxism, implicitly targeting the Kautskyan model
of mechanical-evolutionary determinism in which historical laws operate
independently of proletarian subjectivity. Korsch similarly charged Second
International orthodoxy with reducing Marxism to a positivist natural
science of society. Anderson's Considerations on Western Marxism (1976)
canonized this narrative: Western Marxism defined itself precisely against
the "Kautskyan" inheritance of economistic fatalism and political passivity.
Kolakowski's Main Currents of Marxism (1978) offered the most thoroughgoing
philosophical critique, arguing Kautsky's evolutionism collapsed historical
materialism into social Darwinism and left no room for revolutionary agency.

The second phase, beginning in the 1980s and intensifying after 2000,
involves revisionist rehabilitation. Salvadori's Karl Kautsky and the
Socialist Revolution (1979) was the first major scholarly reassessment,
arguing that Kautsky's fatalism was strategically ambiguous rather than
theoretically confused. Steenson's Karl Kautsky, 1854-1938 (1978) offered
a sympathetic intellectual biography. More recently, Blackledge's articles
and book-length treatment have argued that Kautsky's theory of history,
especially before 1910, was sophisticated and that standard critiques
conflate different periods of his thought. Gronow's On the Formation of
Marxism (2016) places Kautsky's theory of capitalism in close dialogue with
Marx and argues against the "vulgar Marxism" caricature. Lewis's review
essay "Marxism after Marx" (2017) surveys the state of the "Kautsky
revival" literature.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain directly addresses how Kautsky's philosophy of history has been
characterized, contested, and reassessed across the twentieth and twenty-
first centuries — providing the essential critical framework for evaluating
his historiosophy. The positivism/scientism charge, the Western Marxist
counter-definition, and the revisionist rehabilitation all bear directly on
the project's central question of what Kautsky's philosophy of history
actually amounts to.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Althusser's specific engagement with Kautsky and Second International
"economism" is under-represented in the journal literature; Colletti's
critique in Marxism and Hegel is cited but not separately searchable as a
journal article. Gramsci's scattered notebook remarks on Kautsky lack a
dedicated secondary study in English.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The debate between the "mechanistic/fatalist" reading and the revisionist
"sophisticated/strategic" reading of Kautsky should be the main organizing
tension. Note that Blackledge and Gronow both qualify the revisionism: they
argue Kautsky's early theory was sophisticated while acknowledging its later
deterioration.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Western Marxist critique (mechanistic/positivist): 4 papers — Lukacs,
  Korsch, Anderson, Kolakowski
- Revisionist rehabilitation: 5 papers — Salvadori, Steenson, Blackledge,
  Gronow, Lewis
- Theory-practice nexus: 2 papers — Shandro, Blackledge (Trotsky)
- Biographical-contextual: 2 papers — Donald, Lewis (review)
====================================================================
}

@book{anderson1976western,
  author = {Anderson, Perry},
  title = {Considerations on Western Marxism},
  year = {1976},
  publisher = {New Left Books},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Anderson argues that Western Marxism — from Lukacs and
  Korsch through Gramsci, the Frankfurt School, and Althusser — constitutes
  a coherent tradition defined by its displacement from political economy to
  philosophy and aesthetics, and its rupture with the working class. The
  book identifies the Second International, and implicitly Kautsky's
  orthodoxy, as the baseline from which Western Marxism departed: the
  Kautskyan model of mechanical economism and passive fatalism is precisely
  what Western Marxism reacted against.

  RELEVANCE: Provides the canonical framework for understanding how
  "Kautskyism" became a negative reference point for twentieth-century
  Marxist philosophy; essential for situating critiques by Lukacs, Korsch,
  and Gramsci within a common intellectual trajectory.

  POSITION: Western Marxist critique; defines Kautsky's model as the foil
  against which critical Marxist philosophy constituted itself.
  },
  keywords = {Western-Marxism, Second-International, critical-reception, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{kolakowski1978main,
  author = {Ko{\l}akowski, Leszek},
  title = {Main Currents of Marxism: Its Rise, Growth, and Dissolution},
  year = {1978},
  publisher = {Clarendon Press},
  address = {Oxford},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The three-volume work includes a sustained philosophical
  critique of Kautsky in Vol. II ("The Golden Age"), arguing that Kautsky
  transformed historical materialism into a form of social Darwinism:
  history operates through necessary evolutionary laws independent of human
  will, socialism is an inevitable natural outcome, and revolutionary agency
  is absorbed into a fatalist waiting strategy. Kolakowski charges that
  Kautsky's "scientism" evacuates normative and dialectical content from
  Marx's thought.

  RELEVANCE: The most philosophically rigorous version of the "positivism"
  charge against Kautsky; any reassessment of Kautsky's philosophy of
  history must engage with Kolakowski's specific arguments about social
  Darwinism, the collapse of teleology into naturalism, and the loss of
  agency.

  POSITION: Philosophical critique from a post-Marxist perspective;
  represents the most sustained indictment of Kautsky's historiosophy.
  },
  keywords = {positivism-critique, scientism, philosophy-of-history, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{salvadori1979kautsky,
  author = {Salvadori, Massimo L.},
  title = {Karl Kautsky and the Socialist Revolution, 1880--1938},
  year = {1979},
  publisher = {New Left Books},
  address = {London},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: The first comprehensive scholarly reassessment of Kautsky's
  political and theoretical trajectory across six decades. Salvadori argues
  that Kautsky's apparent fatalism was less a theoretical confusion than a
  deliberate strategic choice — maintaining a maximalist socialist horizon
  while accepting the constraints of parliamentary reformism. The "passive
  radicalism" of his mature period reflected a conscious calculation about
  the conditions for socialist transformation in Wilhelmine Germany.

  RELEVANCE: Establishes the revisionist framework that later scholars
  (Blackledge, Gronow) build on; reframes the debate from "was Kautsky
  mechanistic?" to "what strategic and political logic underlies his
  apparent determinism?"

  POSITION: Revisionist rehabilitation; treats Kautsky's evolutionism as
  politically motivated rather than theoretically deficient.
  },
  keywords = {revisionist-rehabilitation, political-strategy, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

